---
description: JRebirth boundaries and forbidden shortcuts
alwaysApply: true
---
# JRebirth Boundaries

Always prefer the mechanisms that the framework already provides over ad-hoc shortcuts. JRebirth has thread management, wave-based messaging, and a role split precisely so that you do not need these shortcuts.

## Shortcuts that are banned by default

- **No `new Thread(...)` in normal application flow.** JRebirth routes work through its internal thread manager. Creating raw threads bypasses that manager, breaks concurrency guarantees, and makes the lifecycle uncontrollable. Use a Service instead.

- **No `Platform.runLater(...)` used as a design pattern.** A single `runLater` to fix a one-off edge case may be acceptable, but using it as a recurring pattern signals that UI state is being mutated from the wrong thread. Restructure to let the Model own the state and update it through waves or commands.

- **No heavy business logic placed inside a View or a Controller.** Views build nodes; Controllers wire events. Business rules, validation, and orchestration belong in the Model or in Commands.

- **No stateful bindings owned by a View when they belong to the Model.** If a binding outlives a single render pass or needs to be observed by other components, it belongs in the Model.

- **No `localFacade().globalFacade()` access except in rare, hard integration cases.** Accessing the global facade directly breaks the component isolation that JRebirth provides. Communicate through Waves instead.

## When in doubt

Stop and read the relevant role skill before writing more code. A few minutes reading the skill is cheaper than refactoring a broken design later.
